# Review ownership for the release path and the tooling that drives it.
#
# GitHub consults this file ONLY when it evaluates a pull request's reviews, and ONLY from the
# branch that pull request TARGETS -- never from the default branch. On its own it blocks
# nothing: what gives it force is whichever org ruleset applies to THIS branch, and that is a
# separate object that changes over time. A copy missing from one branch protects nothing there
# and fails SILENTLY, by requesting no review at all, which is why the file exists on each of
# the three branches separately and is kept identical across them.
#
# WHAT THE PATHS BELOW MEAN, as of 2026-08-21. The operator's branch policy requires an admin
# for any pull request into `dev` or `test` that touches CI or scripts, and requires an admin
# to merge into `prod` at all. This file is the whole implementation of the first half: there
# is no ruleset field for "CI or scripts", so the guarded set is exactly what is listed here.
# A path absent from this list is a path any developer may change and merge alone.
#
# It is deliberately BROAD -- all of `/scripts/`, not a subdirectory of it. It owned
# `/scripts/ci/` until 2026-08-21, which read as coverage and was not: it left
# `release-gates/` unowned in cks-game-api, so the gates could be edited by the very change
# they would have refused, and it left `stage-site-publisher-key.sh` and `migrations/`
# unowned in infra-control-plane while owning `deploy-cp.sh` beside them.
#
# The cost is stated rather than discovered: an ordinary change to a helper script now waits on
# the code owner. If that owner cannot keep up, the honest fix is to narrow this list on
# purpose or add owners -- not to bypass it, because a gate bypassed on every run is not a gate.
#
# There are three branches and only three. `main` was deleted on the remote on 2026-08-21 in
# every repository on this model. Three repos still hold a `main` and are deliberately
# untouched: cks-management-api, cks-control-plane and crowded-kingdoms are ARCHIVED, so their
# refs are read-only and tidying one would mean un-archiving it.
#
# Owner: scripts/apply-codeowners.mjs, which DERIVES this file from the repo's own previous
# copy rather than rendering a template -- so a repo-specific rule below is preserved, not
# overwritten. Edit the tool, not the fourteen copies.

/.github/ @michaelmarshall
/scripts/ @michaelmarshall
